An git-annex repository with an autoenable=yes special remote can be used
to exfiltrate data from another git-annex repository.

For example, suppose that ~/annex on my laptop is a git-annex repository
that contains some annexed files whose content I only provide to some
people. But I am not trying to hide that I have those files in that
repository. So I push the git repository, without the annexed files,
to a public server.

All an attacker needs to do is make a git-annex repository that contains an
autoenabled special remote of type git:

    git-annex initremote foo type=git location=/home/joey/annex autoenable=true

(They will first need to dummy up their own /home/joey/annex repository,
with the same uuid as mine; that uuid is discoverable in what I pushed.)

In their repository, they can make some annexed files, with innocuous
names, that use the keys of the annexed files I am keeping private.
And make the git-annex branch record that those keys are located in the
uuid of my repository.

Then they only need to coax me into cloning their repository and running
git-annex sync. The foo remote is autoenabled and sync will get my private
files from ~/annex. And, if the attacker's repository has preferred
content configured, will send them on to origin.

This security hole is not limited to autoenabled git remotes. It could be
an autoenabled S3 bucket, if I habitually keep S3 environment variables
set. Or it could be an autoenabled rsync special remote with an rsyncurl
that points into my server via ssh. Unlike the git special remote,
some of those don't need the UUID to match either.

A harder to exploit variant of this security hole is when I have not
published my ~/annex git repository. The attacker guesses that I am in
possession of a file with a known hash, and guesses where I might be
keeping it. They can try many locations with many autoenabled special
remotes.

(It's almost possible to use this to exfiltrate arbitrary non-annexed files.
The attacker could make a directory or rsync special remote with
importtree=yes that autoenables. The only reason this doesn't work
is that remote.name.annex-tracking-branch needs to be set in order
to make `git-annex sync` use it. They could try to social engineer 
me into running `git-annex import`, but that seems considerably harder.
Still, while this seems too hard to be a viable attack, it seems likely
that any fix to the main attack will also close off this one.) 

--[[Joey]]

## fix checklist

* implement verified remote log, and make RemoteConfig values from it be
  preferred over git-annex branch remote.log (done)
* initremote populate verified remote log (done)
* implement git-annex verifyremote (done)
* enableremote of a new remote should display remote configuration and
  tell user to check it and run git-annex verifyremote (done)
* enableremote of an existing remote should modify the (verified)
  remote configuration as specified by the user, and save it as
  the new verified configuration, and to the git-annex branch
  (done)
* handle upgrades (done)
* When any enabled special remote does not have an entry in the verified
  remote log, disable annex objects transfers to all remotes, to avoid
  annex objects leaking out of it to other remotes. (done)
* What to do about already running processes that could send annex objects
  that get received from the special remote on to other remotes?
* Disable all git pushes (using git pre-push hook), to avoid
  presence information leaking out of it.
* when using a remote and remote.log has a changed RemoteConfig,
  warn user that the configuration has changed and suggest running
  git-annex verifyremote. How to avoid warning too often or not often
  enough? Or, maybe this is not needed, and the user can decide
  on their own when they want to verify the new configuration?
* Maybe make verifyremote store the config that was shown to the user,
  avoiding the need to copy and paste the config hash.
  But, bear in mind that the current design is safe even if git-annex
  branch changes to the remote configuration arrive, and another process
  runs enableremote, before the user runs `verifyremote --safe`.
  The hash guarantees that what gets verified is what the user saw.
  What should be possible is to have a simple counter, rather than a hash,
  with a log of verification prompts that the counter indexes into.
* verifyremote --unsafe message is incorrect if it is run *after* a remote
  was already verified with --safe. It is also incorrect if it is run
  after upgrading from an old version of git-annex which automatically
  makes the config verified.
